From f3bbfe56d82fb0ba0dabc4a1be6ecf8ee63de3e9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: arthur <arthur.miller@live.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 05:16:47 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 01/13] Use zmij for shortest float printing

Port the zmij binary-to-decimal algorithm (https://github.com/vitaut/zmij,
https://vitaut.net/posts/2025/faster-dtoa/) to src/code/zmij.lisp and use
it for shortest round-trip output of nonzero single and double floats on
64-bit platforms: PRINT-FLOAT, FLONUM-TO-DIGITS and %FLONUM-TO-DIGITS
(FLONUM-EXPONENT). Fixed-precision output (~F, ~E, ~G, ~$), long-float
and 32-bit platforms keep Burger-Dybvig.

The power-of-ten and exponent-shift tables are built at compile time
using integer arithmetic only. All intermediates are typed with exact
ranges, so the conversion neither allocates nor calls generic arithmetic.

Digits are written into a stack-allocated base-string. On x86-64 a new
SSE2 VOP, %ZMIJ-STORE-DIGITS, converts 16 digits at once, stores them
with one MOVDQU and returns a mask of nonzero digits for trimming
leading and trailing zeros. SSE2 is baseline, so no CPU dispatch is
needed. Other platforms use a portable multiply-and-shift BCD version.

Subnormals now print their true shortest form (1.4295402e-39 prints as
1.42954e-39); both forms read back as the same float.
---
